ready: 1
kind: module
id: stacks
name: Stacks
summary: >-
  Owns the Kernle home, the stack registry and stack resolution, owner-only access, configuration,
  and the daemon's lifecycle.
notes: >-
  It also owns credentials and the model binding.
runs_in: [sys.cli, sys.daemon, sys.python]
owns:
  - stack registry (DATA-STACK)
  - configuration and credentials
  - daemon lifecycle
invariants:
  - Every request is refused unless it comes from the OS account that owns the Kernle home.
  - Python, the command-line tool, hooks, the MCP server, and the OpenClaw plugin choose a stack the same way.
  - Exactly one daemon runs per Kernle home, and it is the only writer of stack databases while it runs.
rationale: >-
  Each stack has its own directory and database, so deleting a stack is one
  directory removal and one project's memory is physically separate from
  another's. The registry lives apart from the stack databases because a
  request must find its stack before any stack database is chosen.


  One resolver serves every entry point, because a stack chosen differently by
  hooks and by tools splits one agent's memory in two and lets unrelated
  projects with the same folder name share memory.
